<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jonathan Swift</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1667">1667</a>–<a href="/years/1745">1745</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Dean Swift, Isaac Bickerstaff, M. B. Drapier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jonathan Swift was an Anglo-Irish writer, satirist and Anglican cleric, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in the Kingdom of Ireland. He published his works under pseudonyms such as Isaac Bickerstaff and M. B. Drapier, or anonymously, among them A Tale of a Tub (1704), the prose satire Gulliver's Travels (1726) and A Modest Proposal (1729), whose deadpan irony led such satire to be called &quot;Swiftian&quot;. He joined the Whigs early on, but in 1710 the Tories recruited him as editor of The Examiner. In 1713 he was appointed dean of St Patrick's Cathedral, Dublin. He died in 1745 and was buried in his cathedral beside Esther Johnson; Gulliver's Travels remains the most printed book by an Irish writer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jonathan-swift.jpg" alt="Jonathan Swift" width="330" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Jervas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jonathan_Swift_by_Charles_Jervas_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protestant Ireland produced an intellectual culture of its own: Trinity College Dublin was founded in 1592, and <b>Jonathan Swift</b>, George Berkeley, Oliver Goldsmith and Edmund Burke were among its writers and thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jonathan Swift</b>, later Dean of St Patrick's Cathedral in Dublin, was born.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whoever could make two ears of corn, or two blades of grass, to grow upon a spot of ground, where only one grew before, would deserve better of mankind, and do more essential service to his country, than the whole race of politicians put together.&#8221;</p><footer>Gulliver's Travels (1726), part 2, chapter 7 &#183; original &#183; the King of Brobdingnag's opinion, as Gulliver reports it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_the_Rev._Jonathan_Swift/Volume_6/A_Voyage_to_Brobdingnag/Chapter_7" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is computed that eleven thousand persons have at several times suffered death, rather than submit to break their eggs at the smaller end.&#8221;</p><footer>Gulliver's Travels (1726), part 1, chapter 4 &#183; original &#183; Gulliver is told of Lilliput's long quarrel over which end of an egg to break &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_the_Rev._Jonathan_Swift/Volume_6/A_Voyage_to_Lilliput/Chapter_4" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
